course: dictionary
lesson: 9554
title: Stork: a bird with a long story
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A stork is a large bird with long legs, a long neck and a long straight beak. Stork is a noun, so you can have one stork or two storks.
explain: The dictionary tells you what storks do: they wade in wet places and eat fish, frogs and insects. Context can give you the same clue: «In summer Sashko visits his granny's village, where there's a leleka, a stork, nesting on a tall pole or roof.» The words after the comma tell you that leleka is the Ukrainian word for stork.
explain: A word can mean different things in different places. In science lessons a stork is a real bird that flies long distances. In old stories and cartoons a stork is said to bring babies, but this is a fairy tale, not true. Ask yourself: is this sentence about real life or about make-believe?
fact: A stork is a large bird with long legs, a long neck and a long straight beak.
fact: Storks wade in wet places and eat fish, frogs and insects.
fact: White storks fly long distances between Europe and Africa each year.
fact: Storks build huge nests on roofs and chimneys, and they can nest on tall poles.
fact: The idea that a stork brings babies to families is a fairy tale, not true.
exercise: «White storks fly long distances between Europe and Africa each year.» What does long distances mean? | Nearby in the garden | Lots of tiny steps | *Far apart | Very short walks
exercise: In old stories a stork is said to bring babies. Is this true? | Only in science lessons | Yes, storks always bring babies to homes | *No, it is a fairy tale | Yes, but only in Germany
exercise: «Warm air hardly rises over cold sea, so Ukrainian storks go round the Black Sea, not across it.» Why do the storks go round? | They like the taste of the water | *Warm air hardly rises over cold sea, so flying across is hard | They are afraid of boats | Storks cannot fly over land
test: «In 1822 a stork in Germany was found with an African spear in its neck.» What does this help show about storks? | They only eat frogs | They sleep in mud all winter | They never leave Germany | *They spend winter in Africa
test: «White storks are almost silent and clatter their bills to greet each other.» What does clatter mean here? | To fly away quietly | To sing a long, soft song | To eat quickly | *To make a rattling noise by knocking the bill
exam: «Bringing missing animals back is called rewilding, and in Britain it has brought back beavers, white storks and nightingales.» What does rewilding mean? | Building a zoo for birds | Sending animals to live in Africa | Painting animals in bright colours | *Bringing missing animals back and letting nature run itself again
